{\today}\\
UC Berkeley, Berkeley Institute for Data Science (BIDS)\\
Berkeley, CA

\vspace*{0.6\baselineskip}

Dear BIDS Hiring Committee,

\vspace*{0.3\baselineskip}

As a Research Software Engineer at OHSU's Knight Cancer Institute, I lead development of Funnel, an open-source task-orchestration toolkit that runs genomic analysis workloads across cloud and HPC backends, and I've spent my career embedded in the kind of multi-institution open source consortia --- GA4GH, in my case --- that this role is built around. I don't have direct JupyterHub experience, but I'd welcome the chance to bring that same class of server-side orchestration infrastructure and open source community practice to BIDS as an Open Source Research Software Engineer on BioJupyter.

\paragraph{\textbf{\scshape Experience}}\

\vspace*{0.3\baselineskip}

\begin{itemize}[itemsep=0pt, topsep=1pt]

\item Lead developer of Funnel, which executes genomic analysis tasks across cloud and HPC environments --- including Slurm and Kubernetes --- through a standard task-execution API; wrote the Kubernetes deployment templates and Helm charts used to run it and a research data commons in production, plus CI/CD across AWS, Azure, and OpenStack.

\item Built the Nextflow plugin (nf-ga4gh) and Python client library (py-tes) around the GA4GH Task Execution API, giving genomic pipelines a common interface across execution backends without rewriting pipeline code.

\item Coordinate task dependencies and API design with external \link{https://www.ga4gh.org/}{GA4GH} consortium partners across institutions --- distributed decision-making, consensus-building, and issue/PR-based code review, the practices multi-stakeholder projects like Jupyter run on.

\item Lead developer of the FHIR-Aggregator client, built on HL7 FHIR for the NIH Cloud Platform Interoperability program, unifying clinical and genomic data under a standard schema --- experience with complex, multi-modal biomedical datasets and open standards.

\item Bioinformatics background as a research software developer on Reactome, an open-source pathways database, plus a B.S. in Biology --- domain fluency for BioJupyter's biomedical researchers.

\end{itemize}

\paragraph{\textsc{GitHub Repositories}}\

\vspace*{0.3\baselineskip}

\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0pt, topsep=1pt]

\item \link{https://github.com/calypr/funnel}{github.com/calypr/funnel} --- lead developer; issue triage, PR review, release management

\item \link{https://github.com/FHIR-Aggregator/fhir-aggregator-client}{github.com/FHIR-Aggregator/fhir-aggregator-client} --- lead developer, NIH Cloud Platform Interoperability program

\item \link{https://github.com/ohsu-comp-bio/py-tes}{github.com/ohsu-comp-bio/py-tes} --- maintainer, GA4GH TES Python client

\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{\textsc{Computational Infrastructure}}\

\vspace*{0.3\baselineskip}

\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0pt, topsep=1pt]

\item \link{https://ohsu-comp-bio.github.io/funnel/docs/compute/kubernetes/}{Funnel's Kubernetes compute backend} --- schedules containerized tasks onto a Kubernetes cluster

\item \link{https://ohsu-comp-bio.github.io/funnel/docs/compute/slurm/}{Funnel's Slurm compute backend} --- schedules the same tasks onto an HPC cluster via Slurm

\item \link{https://github.com/nextflow-io/nf-ga4gh}{nf-ga4gh} --- the GA4GH TES executor plugin that lets Nextflow dispatch pipeline tasks to either backend above

\end{enumerate}

Best,\\

Liam Beckman

